.SH NAME wyckoff - An introduction to Richard D. Wyckoff's market theory

.SH DESCRIPTION Richard D. Wyckoff's Market Theory is a method of analyzing financial markets through price action, trading volume, supply, and demand.

Developed by Richard D. Wyckoff, the method attempts to understand the behavior of large market participants, commonly referred to as the Composite Operator.

The central idea is that market movements are not entirely random. By studying price and volume behavior, traders can attempt to identify accumulation, distribution, and changes in market direction.

.SH THE COMPOSITE OPERATOR The Composite Operator is a theoretical representation of large, informed market participants.

Wyckoff suggested that traders should imagine the market as if it were controlled by a single powerful operator.

This operator accumulates assets when prices are relatively low, raises prices when demand exceeds supply, distributes holdings at higher prices, and eventually allows prices to decline.

The Composite Operator is a conceptual model, not necessarily a single individual or institution.

.SH THE THREE FUNDAMENTAL LAWS

.SS 1. Law of Supply and Demand Price movements are determined by the relationship between supply and demand.

When demand exceeds supply, prices tend to rise.

When supply exceeds demand, prices tend to fall.

When supply and demand are approximately balanced, prices tend to move sideways.

.SS 2. Law of Cause and Effect A period of accumulation or distribution creates the conditions for a subsequent price movement.

The longer and more significant the accumulation or distribution period, the greater the potential subsequent movement.

Wyckoff used Point and Figure charting to estimate potential price objectives.

.SS 3. Law of Effort versus Result Trading volume represents effort, while price movement represents result.

When increasing volume produces significant price movement, effort and result are generally in agreement.

When substantial volume produces little price movement, it may indicate absorption, resistance, or a potential change in direction.

.SH THE FOUR MARKET PHASES

.SS 1. Accumulation Accumulation occurs when large market participants gradually purchase assets after a prolonged decline.

During this phase, prices generally move within a trading range.

Supply is gradually absorbed, and selling pressure may weaken.

Important events include:

.IP (bu 2 Preliminary Support (PS) .IP (bu 2 Selling Climax (SC) .IP (bu 2 Automatic Rally (AR) .IP (bu 2 Secondary Test (ST) .IP (bu 2 Spring .IP (bu 2 Sign of Strength (SOS) .IP (bu 2 Last Point of Support (LPS)

The phase is generally considered complete when demand becomes dominant and price breaks above the trading range.

.SS 2. Markup Markup is the upward trend that follows successful accumulation.

Demand exceeds supply, resulting in higher prices.

The market typically forms higher highs and higher lows.

Temporary corrections may occur as the market advances.

These corrections can provide opportunities to identify additional areas of support.

.SS 3. Distribution Distribution occurs when large market participants gradually sell their holdings after an extended upward movement.

Prices generally move within a trading range while supply begins to overcome demand.

Important events include:

.IP (bu 2 Preliminary Supply (PSY) .IP (bu 2 Buying Climax (BC) .IP (bu 2 Automatic Reaction (AR) .IP (bu 2 Secondary Test (ST) .IP (bu 2 Upthrust (UT) .IP (bu 2 Sign of Weakness (SOW) .IP (bu 2 Last Point of Supply (LPSY)

The phase is generally considered complete when supply becomes dominant and price breaks below the trading range.

.SS 4. Markdown Markdown is the downward trend that follows distribution.

Supply exceeds demand, causing prices to decline.

The market generally forms lower highs and lower lows.

Temporary rallies may occur, but the overall market structure remains bearish until demand begins to regain control.

.SH IMPORTANT WYCKOFF CONCEPTS

.SS Spring A Spring occurs when price temporarily moves below the support of an accumulation trading range before recovering back above it.

It can indicate that available supply has been absorbed and that a potential upward movement is developing.

.SS Upthrust An Upthrust occurs when price temporarily moves above the resistance of a distribution trading range before falling back below it.

It can indicate a failed breakout and potential weakness.

.SS Sign of Strength (SOS) A Sign of Strength is a strong upward price movement that suggests demand is becoming dominant.

It is commonly identified by an upward movement accompanied by increased volume and improved price performance.

.SS Sign of Weakness (SOW) A Sign of Weakness is a downward price movement that suggests supply is becoming dominant.

It may indicate that distribution is progressing or that the market is transitioning into markdown.

.SS Last Point of Support (LPS) An LPS is a reaction or pullback following a Sign of Strength.

It represents an area where selling pressure appears limited and demand may be returning.

.SS Last Point of Supply (LPSY) An LPSY is a weak rally following a Sign of Weakness.

It represents an area where buying pressure appears limited and supply may be returning.

.SH PRICE AND VOLUME ANALYSIS

Wyckoff analysis relies heavily on the relationship between price movement and trading volume.

Common observations include:

.IP (bu 2 Increasing volume with rising prices may indicate strong demand. .IP (bu 2 Increasing volume with falling prices may indicate strong supply. .IP (bu 2 Declining volume during a pullback may indicate reduced selling pressure. .IP (bu 2 High volume with limited price movement may indicate absorption. .IP (bu 2 Low volume during a breakout may suggest insufficient participation.

These observations must be interpreted in the context of the overall market structure.

.SH THE WYCKOFF MARKET CYCLE

The four market phases form a continuous theoretical cycle:

.nf ACCUMULATION | v MARKUP | v DISTRIBUTION | v MARKDOWN | v ACCUMULATION .fi

The cycle illustrates how markets may transition between periods of consolidation, upward movement, distribution, and downward movement.

Not every market follows this cycle perfectly.

.SH PRACTICAL APPLICATION

When applying Wyckoff analysis, traders generally:

.IP 1. 4 Identify the current market trend. .IP 2. 4 Determine whether the market is accumulating or distributing. .IP 3. 4 Analyze price and volume behavior. .IP 4. 4 Identify important support and resistance levels. .IP 5. 4 Look for confirmation through events such as Springs, Upthrusts, Signs of Strength, and Signs of Weakness. .IP 6. 4 Evaluate whether the market is transitioning into a new phase.

A single event should not be treated as definitive confirmation. Multiple observations should support the interpretation.

.SH LIMITATIONS

Wyckoff Theory is an analytical framework, not a guaranteed method of predicting future prices.

Market structures can be ambiguous, and similar price patterns may have different interpretations depending on their context.

Volume data can also vary between exchanges and financial instruments.

Traders should combine market structure analysis with appropriate risk management.

